From 8f5db9dc551b96f48b33d921697531a140520cf5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 16:19:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11085/11362] Record that a spent cascade stays on the
 dashboard as a permanent banner

The cascade line has shown the same finding for two consecutive hours. Its one
dependent is already blocked, so the cascade is spent, and it will keep printing
hourly until milestone 612 is archived and exp6987 leaves the active roadmap.

A line headed PENDING CASCADE that is permanently true is a banner rather than a
signal. Readers learn to skip it, and the next genuine cascade lands on a line
nobody reads -- the cry-wolf failure this project's own guidance names as worse
than the gap it closes.

Not an implementation oversight: the check annotates burned dependents on
purpose so that pending reads as pending. The gap is that annotating is not
suppressing, and when every dependent carries the annotation the finding has no
live consequence left.

Fix shape recorded rather than taken, because it deserves an operator's view:
suppress a fully-spent cascade, or report it once under a heading that is not
PENDING. There is a reasonable counter-argument that a standing reminder of an
unrepaired root is exactly the point, and I do not want to silence a guard on my
own judgement three hours after asking for it.

Worth noting how it was found: by watching the check run twice, not by testing
it. Its six tests cover firing and silence correctly, and none of them describes
what the output should look like on the second hour of an unchanged condition,
because that is a property of repetition rather than of one evaluation.

